From 55f532644a25cfb2b4f46b6e2d79a9f181a3865e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive kernel patches <ceralive-patches@ceralive.tv>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 17:05:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] Start the HDMI-RX audio domain from the capture lifecycle

Origin: CeraLive HDMI-RX audio capture investigation, Rock 5B+, 2026-08-06.

With 0001-0003 applied, /dev/snd/pcmC3D0c registers, opens and negotiates
hw_params, and then every read() returns EIO. 0004 made that failure printable
for the first time, and the board output it produced is what this patch is
built on:

  rockchip-i2s-tdm fddf8000.i2s: hw_params[capture]: dma addr=0x...fddf8028
      addr_width=4 maxburst=16 ch=2 rate=44100 sample_bits=16
  rockchip-i2s-tdm fddf8000.i2s: capture dma on: DMACR=0x10f0010 XFER=0x0
      INTSR=0x0 RXFIFOLR=0x0
  rk-hdmi-sound hdmiin-sound: capture transfer timed out (DMA or IRQ trouble?):
      state=3 hw_ptr=0 appl_ptr=0 avail=0 twake=441 period=441 buffer=2205
  rk-hdmi-sound hdmiin-sound: capture xfer failed: err=-5 state=3 hw_ptr=0

The DMA request line is armed (DMACR RDE set), the descriptor is submitted, and
hw_ptr never moves. Nothing ever reaches the FIFO.

That is not an I2S-side failure. rockchip_i2s_tdm_trigger() -> _start() calls
rockchip_i2s_tdm_dma_ctrl() and then rockchip_i2s_tdm_xfer_start(), which for a
non-TRCM capture stream does write I2S_XFER_RXS_START; clk_trcm is 0 here
because rk_hdmi_parse_daifmt() makes hdmirx_ctrler the bit-clock and frame
master and rockchip_i2s_tdm_set_fmt() drops trcm for slave mode. The XFER=0x0
in the log above is read INSIDE dma_ctrl(), i.e. before that write, and
I2S_XFER is a non-volatile register absent from reg_defaults under
REGCACHE_FLAT, so that read returned the regmap cache rather than the register.
It never said the RX enable bit was unset. The last hunk below fixes that
measurement so the next board run cannot be misread the same way.

The receiver is enabled. The bus feeding it is idle, and rk_hdmirx.c says why.

The HDMI-RX audio domain is gated by two bits: GLOBAL_SWENABLE.AUDIO_ENABLE and
AUDIO_PROC_CONFIG0.I2S_EN. hdmirx_audio_setup() explicitly CLEARS AUDIO_ENABLE
and never touches I2S_EN. Both are only ever set from
hdmirx_delayed_work_audio() -- AUDIO_ENABLE at its top, I2S_EN via
hdmirx_enable_audio_output(). Until that work has run, the controller drives no
BCLK, no LRCK and no SDATA, so i2s7_8ch clocks in nothing regardless of what
the ALSA side does. Empty FIFO, hw_ptr stuck at 0, EIO out of wait_for_avail().

And nothing in the capture path starts that work. Its only in-kernel trigger is
avpunit_1_int_handler() on DEFRAMER_VSYNC_THR_REACHED_IRQ, which masks its own
source off after the first delivery, so it is one-shot and depends on the
hdmi_irq being delivered at all. Its only other trigger is the vendor
RK_HDMIRX_CMD_SET_AUDIO_STATE ioctl, which is a rk_hdmirx-specific V4L2
private command that no ALSA client issues. snd_pcm_open(), hw_params and
trigger START all leave the audio domain exactly as hdmirx_audio_setup() left
it: off. The PCM lifecycle and the audio-domain lifecycle were simply never
connected.

CORRECTION TO THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS PATCH
=============================================

The first version of this patch connected the two lifecycles by having
hdmirx_audio_startup() force the work to run and then WAIT FOR IT with
flush_delayed_work(). That was wrong, and wrong in the worst possible way: it
deadlocked on precisely the path where it worked.

  * hdmi_codec_startup() (sound/soc/codecs/hdmi-codec.c) takes hcp->lock and
    calls ops->audio_startup -- i.e. hdmirx_audio_startup() -- with that mutex
    HELD, releasing it only at the end of the function.
  * flush_delayed_work() blocks until hdmirx_delayed_work_audio() has finished.
  * hdmirx_delayed_work_audio()'s SUCCESS path (cur_state != 0, audio not yet
    present) calls hdmirx_audio_handle_plugged_change(), which calls the codec's
    plugged_cb().
  * plugged_cb() takes hcp->lock unconditionally -- not only when the jack state
    actually changes.

So the work could never finish while the flushing thread held hcp->lock, and
the flushing thread could never proceed until the work finished. A capture open
on a source that genuinely had audio would hang in D state, taking the whole
sound card and the HDMI-RX audio domain with it. The failure case (no audio
present) did not hit it, because that path never calls plugged_cb -- which is a
uniquely nasty signature: the "fix" would appear to behave until the moment it
started working.

Two smaller problems came with it:

  * hdmirx_plugout() cancelled delayed_work_audio with the non-synchronous
    cancel_delayed_work(), which cannot stop an already-running instance, and
    the retry loop above could re-queue the work AFTER that cancel. The work
    then ran on into (or after) the register teardown. hdmirx_remove() and the
    probe error path had the same non-synchronous pattern, which in the remove
    case means writing to a device that is being freed.
  * audio_present was read and written as a plain bool from several contexts.

This version fixes all three.

  * hdmirx_audio_startup() no longer waits on the work item at all. It waits on
    a new completion, audio_ready, which hdmirx_delayed_work_audio() signals
    BEFORE it calls hdmirx_audio_handle_plugged_change(). Publishing readiness
    ahead of the lock-taking callback is what breaks the cycle: the waiter no
    longer needs the worker to FINISH, only to REACH the point where the audio
    domain is up, and reaching that point requires no lock the waiter holds.
    The wait is wait_for_completion_timeout(), 5 x 20 ms, the same 100 ms budget
    as before and still inside the 50 ms settle time the DT documents for this
    port. Nothing in the new wait can block on hcp->lock, work_lock, or the work
    item, so there is no cycle left to close.

  * A lifecycle gate, audio_arm_allowed under audio_arm_lock, decides whether
    the retry loop may arm the work at all. Teardown clears it under that lock
    and only THEN issues cancel_delayed_work_sync(). That ordering is the fix,
    not the sync cancel on its own: once the flag is clear no further arm can
    happen, and any arm that did happen necessarily completed before the cancel
    began, so the cancel is guaranteed to observe and wait out the work it
    queued. hdmirx_audio_startup() stops retrying the moment arming is refused,
    so a capture open concurrent with an unplug cannot resurrect the work behind
    the teardown's back. The gate reopens in hdmirx_audio_setup() (and on
    AUDIO_ON), so a plugout -> plugin cycle recovers.

    hdmirx_plugout() now disarms FIRST, before it tears the registers down,
    rather than most of the way through: the work writes GLOBAL_SWENABLE and
    AUDIO_PROC_CONFIG0 and calls back into hdmi-codec, so letting it overlap
    the teardown was a live-hardware race in its own right. It disarms via
    hdmirx_audio_set_state(AUDIO_OFF) - the driver's own teardown entry point -
    which additionally clears GLOBAL_SWENABLE.AUDIO_ENABLE. An unplug never
    used to clear it (only the AUDIO_OFF ioctl did), so the audio domain stayed
    powered up with no source attached until the next plugin happened to run
    hdmirx_audio_setup(). audio_present is cleared there too, so a capture open
    after a replug no longer takes the "already up" fast path against a domain
    that is in fact down.

    hdmirx_remove(), the probe error path and hdmirx_runtime_suspend() go
    through the same helper, so the audio work is now stopped synchronously and
    un-rearmably on every teardown path.

  * audio_present is accessed with READ_ONCE()/WRITE_ONCE() everywhere it
    crosses contexts -- the work that writes it, and the capture-open callback,
    sysfs audio_present_show() and the RK_V4L2_CID_AUDIO_PRESENT control that
    read it. The completion, not this variable, is the actual synchronisation
    point for the startup wait; the variable is the steady-state answer to "is
    audio up", including for the fast path that skips the wait entirely.

    Note the write to audio_present now precedes the plugged-change callback in
    both branches of the work. That is deliberate and is part of the same
    ordering: readiness is published before any callback that can block.

Lock ordering, stated explicitly because this is what the first version got
wrong: the driver's existing order is work_lock -> hcp->lock (hook_plugged_cb
and hdmirx_plugout both take work_lock and then call into plugged_cb).
hdmirx_audio_startup() runs with hcp->lock held and takes NEITHER work_lock nor
anything that waits on a work_lock holder -- only audio_arm_lock, a leaf
spinlock, and a timed completion. The existing order is therefore preserved and
no inversion is introduced.

The other half of the connection is unchanged from the first version:
hdmirx_plugin() and hdmirx_delayed_work_res_change(), on the paths that have
just confirmed lock and timing, arm the state machine directly via
hdmirx_audio_set_state() rather than relying on the one-shot deframer interrupt.
Those are fire-and-forget schedules, not waits, so they never had the deadlock;
re-arming an already-queued work is a no-op, so they remain purely additive.

The i2s-tdm hunk is diagnostic, not behavioural, and is deliberately additive to
0004 rather than a replacement for it: the pre-start read stays, and a post-start
read is added next to it. It prints I2S_XFER (cached, therefore the driver's
intent) beside INTSR and RXFIFOLR (volatile, therefore hardware), which is what
distinguishes "the receiver was never enabled" from "the receiver is enabled and
the bus is idle" -- the exact distinction this patch turns on.

0004's instrumentation is retained in full so the fix can be confirmed on
hardware: a working capture must now show hw_ptr advancing, RXS=1 with a
non-zero RXFIFOLR, and no "capture xfer failed" line at all.
---
 .../platform/rockchip/hdmirx/rk_hdmirx.c      | 192 +++++++++++++++++-
 sound/soc/rockchip/rockchip_i2s_tdm.c         |  25 +++
 2 files changed, 206 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/hdmirx/rk_hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/hdmirx/rk_hdmirx.c
index a2ec7fc6c..880a36cfc 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/hdmirx/rk_hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/hdmirx/rk_hdmirx.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/completion.h>
 #include <linux/cpufreq.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
@@ -81,6 +82,8 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(low_latency, "low_latency en(0-1)");
 #define NO_LOCK_CFG_RETRY_TIME		300
 #define WAIT_LOCK_STABLE_TIME		20
 #define WAIT_AVI_PKT_TIME		300
+#define HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_TRIES	5
+#define HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_SETTLE_MS	20
 #define LOGIC_CPU_ID_1			1
 #define LOGIC_CPU_ID_2			2
 #define LOGIC_CPU_ID_5			5
@@ -229,6 +232,22 @@ struct rk_hdmirx_dev {
 	struct dentry *debugfs_dir;
 	struct freq_qos_request min_sta_freq_req;
 	struct hdmirx_audiostate audio_state;
+	/*
+	 * Signalled by hdmirx_delayed_work_audio() the moment the audio domain
+	 * comes up, and waited on (bounded) by hdmirx_audio_startup(). It is
+	 * deliberately completed BEFORE the work calls back into hdmi-codec:
+	 * see the comment in hdmirx_delayed_work_audio().
+	 */
+	struct completion audio_ready;
+	/*
+	 * Gate for arming delayed_work_audio from hdmirx_audio_startup(), which
+	 * runs in the caller's process context and must not be allowed to
+	 * re-queue the work behind a teardown's back. audio_arm_lock is the
+	 * whole ordering: an arm can only land while the flag is still set,
+	 * i.e. strictly before the synchronous cancel that follows clearing it.
+	 */
+	spinlock_t audio_arm_lock;
+	bool audio_arm_allowed;
 	struct extcon_dev *extcon;
 	struct hdmirx_cec *cec;
 	struct hdmirx_fence_context fence_ctx;
@@ -3345,6 +3364,55 @@ static void hdmirx_interrupts_setup(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev, bool en)
 	sip_hdmirx_config(HDMIRX_REG_PRE_FETCH, 0, AVPUNIT_1_INT_MASK_N, 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Arm the audio state machine to run immediately, but only while the audio
+ * domain is supposed to be alive.
+ *
+ * Returns false when a teardown has already claimed the domain, in which case
+ * the caller must NOT keep retrying: the synchronous cancel in
+ * hdmirx_audio_disarm_work() is running (or about to), and re-queueing behind
+ * it would leave the work touching an unplugged - or, from hdmirx_remove(), a
+ * freed - device.
+ */
+static bool hdmirx_audio_arm_work(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
+{
+	bool armed = false;
+
+	spin_lock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+	if (hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_allowed) {
+		mod_delayed_work_on(hdmirx_dev->bound_cpu, system_wq,
+				    &hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_audio, 0);
+		armed = true;
+	}
+	spin_unlock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+
+	return armed;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Close the gate, THEN cancel synchronously. That order is the whole point:
+ * once the flag is clear under audio_arm_lock no further arm can happen, and
+ * any arm that did happen necessarily completed before this cancel started, so
+ * the cancel is guaranteed to observe and wait out the work it queued. The
+ * reverse order (cancel, then clear) leaves a window in which a concurrent
+ * hdmirx_audio_startup() re-arms work that nothing will ever cancel.
+ */
+static void hdmirx_audio_disarm_work(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
+{
+	spin_lock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+	hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_allowed = false;
+	spin_unlock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_audio);
+}
+
+static void hdmirx_audio_allow_arm(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
+{
+	spin_lock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+	hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_allowed = true;
+	spin_unlock(&hdmirx_dev->audio_arm_lock);
+}
+
 static void hdmirx_plugin(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -3376,10 +3444,23 @@ static void hdmirx_plugin(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
 	hdmirx_interrupts_setup(hdmirx_dev, true);
 	extcon_set_state_sync(hdmirx_dev->extcon, EXTCON_JACK_VIDEO_IN, true);
 	hdmirx_audio_handle_plugged_change(hdmirx_dev, 1);
+	hdmirx_audio_set_state(hdmirx_dev, AUDIO_ON);
 }
 
 static void hdmirx_plugout(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Stop the audio state machine FIRST, and synchronously. It writes
+	 * GLOBAL_SWENABLE/AUDIO_PROC_CONFIG0 and calls back into hdmi-codec, so
+	 * letting it run on into the register teardown below is a live-hardware
+	 * race - and the old non-synchronous cancel_delayed_work() here could
+	 * not stop an already-running instance at all. AUDIO_OFF is the driver's
+	 * own teardown entry point and also clears GLOBAL_SWENABLE.AUDIO_ENABLE,
+	 * which an unplug never used to do: the audio domain stayed powered up
+	 * with no source until the next plugin's hdmirx_audio_setup().
+	 */
+	hdmirx_audio_set_state(hdmirx_dev, AUDIO_OFF);
+	WRITE_ONCE(hdmirx_dev->audio_present, false);
 	hdmirx_audio_handle_plugged_change(hdmirx_dev, 0);
 	extcon_set_state_sync(hdmirx_dev->extcon, EXTCON_JACK_VIDEO_IN, false);
 	hdmirx_update_bits(hdmirx_dev, SCDC_CONFIG, POWERPROVIDED, 0);
@@ -3400,7 +3481,6 @@ static void hdmirx_plugout(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
 			HDMI_DISABLE);
 	hdmirx_writel(hdmirx_dev, PHYCREG_CONFIG0, 0x0);
 	cancel_delayed_work(&hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_res_change);
-	cancel_delayed_work(&hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_audio);
 	cpu_latency_qos_update_request(&hdmirx_dev->pm_qos, PM_QOS_DEFAULT_VALUE);
 	hdmirx_cancel_cpu_limit_freq(hdmirx_dev);
 	cancel_delayed_work(&hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_heartbeat);
@@ -3599,10 +3679,11 @@ static void hdmirx_audio_set_state(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev, enum audio_
 {
 	switch (stat) {
 	case AUDIO_OFF:
-		cancel_delayed_work_sync(&hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_audio);
+		hdmirx_audio_disarm_work(hdmirx_dev);
 		hdmirx_update_bits(hdmirx_dev, GLOBAL_SWENABLE, AUDIO_ENABLE, 0);
 		break;
 	case AUDIO_ON:
+		hdmirx_audio_allow_arm(hdmirx_dev);
 		schedule_delayed_work_on(hdmirx_dev->bound_cpu,
 					 &hdmirx_dev->delayed_work_audio, HZ / 2);
 		break;
@@ -3635,6 +3716,13 @@ static void hdmirx_audio_setup(struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev)
 		      AFIFO_THR_LOW_QST(0x20) | AFIFO_THR_HIGH_QST(0x160));
 	hdmirx_writel(hdmirx_dev, AUDIO_FIFO_MUTE_THR,
 		      AFIFO_THR_MUTE_LOW_QST(0x8) | AFIFO_THR_MUTE_HIGH_QST(0x178));
+	/*
+	 * The audio hardware is configured; from here until plugout the state
+	 * machine may be armed, including from a capture open. Re-opening the
+	 * gate here (rather than only in hdmirx_audio_set_state()) is what makes
+	 * a plugout -> plugin cycle recover: plugout closes it permanently.
+	 */
+	hdmirx_audio_allow_arm(hdmirx_dev);
 }
 
 static int hdmirx_audio_hw_params(struct device *dev, void *data,
@@ -3648,10 +3736,78 @@ static int hdmirx_audio_hw_params(struct device *dev, void *data,
 static int hdmirx_audio_startup(struct device *dev, void *data)
 {
 	struct rk_hdmirx_dev *hdmirx_dev = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int i;
 
-	if (tx_5v_power_present(hdmirx_dev) && hdmirx_dev->audio_present)
+	if (!tx_5v_power_present(hdmirx_dev)) {
+		dev_dbg(dev, "%s: no source connected\n", __func__);
 		return 0;
-	dev_dbg(dev, "%s: device is no connected or audio is off\n", __func__);
+	}
+
+	if (READ_ONCE(hdmirx_dev->audio_present))
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The HDMI-RX audio domain is gated by GLOBAL_SWENABLE.AUDIO_ENABLE and
+	 * AUDIO_PROC_CONFIG0.I2S_EN. hdmirx_audio_setup() explicitly CLEARS
+	 * AUDIO_ENABLE and never sets I2S_EN; both are only ever set by
+	 * hdmirx_delayed_work_audio(). Until that work has run, the controller
+	 * drives no BCLK/LRCK/SDATA at all, so the i2s7 receiver it feeds sees
+	 * an idle bus no matter what the ALSA side does.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing in the capture lifecycle used to start that work: its only
+	 * in-kernel trigger is avpunit_1_int_handler()'s one-shot
+	 * DEFRAMER_VSYNC_THR_REACHED_IRQ, which masks itself off after the
+	 * first delivery, and its only other trigger is the out-of-tree
+	 * RK_HDMIRX_CMD_SET_AUDIO_STATE ioctl that no generic ALSA client
+	 * issues. Open the capture PCM without either and every read() waits on
+	 * a FIFO that can never fill, which surfaces as -EIO from
+	 * wait_for_avail() with hw_ptr still at 0.
+	 *
+	 * So make the capture open itself start the audio domain, and wait for
+	 * it here rather than returning into a stream that is about to time
+	 * out: this callback runs in process context from hdmi_codec_startup(),
+	 * long before trigger(), and the DT wait time for this port is 50 ms.
+	 *
+	 * CRITICAL: wait on the completion, NEVER on the work item itself.
+	 * hdmi_codec_startup() calls this with hcp->lock HELD, and the work's
+	 * success path calls back into hdmi-codec's plugged_cb(), which takes
+	 * that same hcp->lock unconditionally. Waiting for the work to FINISH
+	 * (flush_delayed_work(), cancel_..._sync(), flush_work()) therefore
+	 * deadlocks outright: the work can never reach its own end while we
+	 * hold the lock it needs. The completion is signalled BEFORE that
+	 * callback, so reaching it never requires hcp->lock, and the wait is
+	 * bounded anyway.
+	 *
+	 * Serialisation: hcp->lock also makes this callback single-threaded per
+	 * codec (hdmi_codec_startup() additionally rejects a second simultaneous
+	 * stream via hcp->busy), so reinit_completion() here cannot race another
+	 * waiter, and a stale completion left over from a previous open cannot
+	 * be mistaken for this open's readiness.
+	 */
+	dev_info(dev, "%s: audio domain idle at capture open, starting it\n",
+		 __func__);
+
+	reinit_completion(&hdmirx_dev->audio_ready);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_TRIES; i++) {
+		if (!hdmirx_audio_arm_work(hdmirx_dev)) {
+			dev_dbg(dev, "%s: audio domain torn down, not re-arming\n",
+				__func__);
+			return 0;
+		}
+		if (wait_for_completion_timeout(&hdmirx_dev->audio_ready,
+				msecs_to_jiffies(HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_SETTLE_MS)))
+			break;
+		if (READ_ONCE(hdmirx_dev->audio_present))
+			break;
+	}
+
+	if (!READ_ONCE(hdmirx_dev->audio_present))
+		dev_warn(dev,
+			 "%s: audio domain did not come up in %d ms; capture will be silent\n",
+			 __func__,
+			 HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_TRIES * HDMIRX_AUDIO_START_SETTLE_MS);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -3823,18 +3979,28 @@ static void hdmirx_delayed_work_audio(struct work_struct *work)
 	if (cur_state != 0) {
 		if (!hdmirx_dev->audio_present) {
 			dev_info(hdmirx_dev->dev, "audio on");
+			/*
+			 * Publish readiness BEFORE calling back into
